{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe Gerrit host (`i-00880b2c7f13527c5`, `t4g.large`) became totally unresponsive for ~28 minutes on\n2026-09-05. A soft reboot did NOT clear it; a stop/start recovered it in under 3 minutes. **The\ncause is NOT established**, and the reason it is not established is a specific, fixable telemetry\ngap.\n\n## Timeline (UTC)\n\n```\n~22:0x   intermittent HTTP 504 on git fetch (nginx Gerrit location on the 60s default\n         proxy_read_timeout while upload-pack legitimately runs to ~144s — antihuman-beefwitted-zebra)\n 23:13   NetworkIn SPIKE: 9.6 MB avg vs ~1.0 MB baseline (10x)\n 23:19   CI checkout for change 2689 fails with the 504 signature\n 23:26   SSM agent last ping\n 23:28   NetworkIn COLLAPSES to 1,829 bytes / 5-min period and stays there\n ~23:3x  TLS handshakes stop completing; TCP connect still succeeds (0.085s)\n ~23:4x  TCP connect itself times out\n 23:47   soft reboot issued — INEFFECTIVE (SSM still Undeliverable, TLS still dead)\n 23:51   stop/start issued\n 23:54   RECOVERED: http=200, TLS 0.18s, git ls-remote exit 0, SSM PingStatus Online\n```\n\nFailure order was TLS -> SSM -> TCP: services degraded before the kernel stopped accepting.\n\n## RULED OUT, with data\n\n- **EBS burst exhaustion.** `EBSIOBalance%` fell only to **61.6%** and `EBSByteBalance%` to\n  **65.2%**; neither approached zero. Recovered to 99% after restart.\n- **CPU saturation.** `CPUUtilization` FELL through the incident: 70 -> 68 -> 60 -> 47 -> 18 -> 24\n  -> 28%. A CPU pin looks nothing like this. (`CPUCreditBalance` was 0.00 and\n  `CPUSurplusCreditBalance` ~361, so the instance was throttled to its 30% baseline — utilisation\n  reading ~28% is consistent with work QUEUING behind a cap, not with CPU being the binding cause.)\n- **Hypervisor-visible failure.** `StatusCheckFailed` was **0.00** for the entire window; EC2\n  reported `running / system ok / instance ok` while the host was completely unreachable.\n- **Disk pressure** — the previously-established failure mode (bug 3276). Root disk was 71-76%\n  against an 85% threshold before the incident. Not implicated.\n\n## The signature, and the leading unmeasured candidate\n\n`NetworkIn` collapsing to ~2 KB per period at 23:28 is the clearest marker — the host stopped\ntransacting. The 10x spike at 23:13, fifteen minutes earlier, is a LEAD and nothing more; it was\nnot correlated to a specific workload.\n\n**Memory is the one major resource AWS does not publish at the hypervisor level.** `rebar-mcp` was\nindependently measured at **4.45 GB RSS (55.6% of 7.8 GiB)** earlier the same evening, squeezing\npage cache to ~600 MB on a git server. Memory exhaustion (OOM, or swap thrash, which presents as\nlow CPU with everything blocked) fits every observation — falling CPU, collapsing network, TLS\nfailing before TCP, SSM agent dying, and a soft reboot failing where a power cycle worked. **It is\nNOT established.** It is the leading candidate precisely because it is the thing we did not measure.\n\n## The telemetry gap — this is the actionable part\n\nWe had EC2 hypervisor metrics only. Everything host-side went dark with the host:\n\n1. **No memory metric survives to CloudWatch.** The rebar probe publishes `mem_used_percent`, but\n   `ignitable-fuchsia-kawala` shows the probe is SIGTERM-ed on its 4-minute timeout with ~42 s of\n   CPU consumed, and its tail metrics degraded to roughly 1 datapoint in 3 hours. So the one\n   memory signal we have is the least reliable thing we publish.\n2. **The SSM agent died at 23:26**, removing the only interactive channel. Diagnosis after that\n   point was impossible.\n3. The reframing that matters: **we do not need telemetry that survives the outage — we need\n   telemetry that captures the RUN-UP.** A memory series with 5-minute resolution for the twenty\n   minutes before 23:28 would very likely settle this. That series should exist and does not,\n   because the publisher truncates.\n\nChange **2684** (`ignitable-fuchsia-kawala`) has since landed and closed after a superseded patchset-1 `Verified +1`, a real patchset-2 `Verified -1`, and a patchset-3 fix. Later monitoring work also added `infra/terraform/monitoring_membound.tf`, including host memory and missing-memory-signal alarms. This ticket therefore does not need to invent a second publisher; the remaining in-scope work is to (1) verify the already-merged memory publisher/alarm path with current tests and (2) update the shell-free recovery runbook so the next total outage can be diagnosed from off-box data rather than SSM access.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 A host memory series reaches CloudWatch reliably enough to show a 20-minute run-up,\n      surviving the probe's truncation behaviour. State whether this rides on 2684's fix or needs\n      an independent publisher, and justify.\n- [ ] AC2 The next occurrence is diagnosable from data alone, without SSM access. Name the specific\n      metrics that would discriminate memory exhaustion from the alternatives.\n- [ ] AC3 An alarm fires on the RUN-UP (e.g. sustained memory above a threshold), not only on the\n      aftermath. An alarm that fires when the host is already gone has no operational value.\n- [ ] AC4 The NetworkIn collapse signature is evaluated as a detector: it is hypervisor-published,\n      so it survives host death. Decide whether it is worth alarming on and record the reasoning.\n- [ ] AC5 The 23:13 NetworkIn spike is attributed to a workload, or explicitly recorded as\n      unattributed.\n\n## Related\n\n`ignitable-fuchsia-kawala` (probe truncation, change 2684), `relevant-dissident-salamander`, `antihuman-beefwitted-zebra`, `witted-invisible-roan`, and `lousy-unsuited-gnu`.\n\n## Implementation plan\n\n- Add a memory-exhaustion / telemetry-survival section to `infra/runbooks/gerrit-host-wedged-ssm-lost.md`, extending the existing wedged-host runbook rather than creating a duplicate runbook. The new section will name the run-up metrics (`mem_available_percent`, `mem_used_percent`, `container_memory_rss_bytes`, `mem_probe_ok`, `container_stats_ok`), the already-merged alarms (`rebar-host-memory-low`, `rebar-mem-signal-absent-while-probe-alive`, `rebar-memory-probe-not-ok`), the alternatives to rule out (`CPUUtilization`, `StatusCheckFailed`, EBS burst/root-disk metrics), the NetworkIn-collapse decision, and the still-unattributed 23:13 NetworkIn spike.\n- Create `tests/unit/test_gerrit_wedged_runbook_memory.py` to pin that runbook content so future edits cannot erase the memory/NetworkIn diagnostic contract.\n- Do not change Terraform unless inspection shows `monitoring_membound.tf` no longer declares the current memory alarms; the current implementation already has the off-box alarm path.\n\n## Testing / Verification\n\n- Run `pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_memory_9ea3.py tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py tests/unit/test_alarm_window_tuning.py -q` to verify the existing publisher/alarm path: memory datapoints are emitted by `observability.sh`, memory probe/container-stats health is surfaced, and the host memory alarms remain wired to actions/windows.\n- Run `pytest tests/unit/test_gerrit_wedged_runbook_memory.py -q` after creating it to prove the identified shell-free runbook file names the memory telemetry survival path, discriminating metrics, NetworkIn-collapse decision, and unattributed 23:13 spike.\n- Run `terraform -chdir=infra/terraform validate` only if Terraform alarm declarations change; otherwise record that no Terraform diff was needed because the already-merged memory alarms satisfy AC3.\n- Run `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null make verify PYTEST_WORKERS=2` before push, dropping to `PYTEST_WORKERS=1` only for a low-memory environmental death.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789014433092144001,"uuid":"4c308000-77cc-4aa8-b9d5-326960711037"}